tical and this  gives you a lot of visibility to see  that very quickly and then do the  investigation so at a use case I saw  recently where the customer was how do I  keep my secrets secret and we may use  cloud kms for this and it's really  important to share you cannot store  secrets inside cloud kms but it's an  enabling technology that will help you  keep your secrets secret the use case  here was API keys so an application that  was being built talked to a third-party  service that authenticate with an API  key in this case it was sending emails  the development a test environment had  one key that had a limited quote or net  limit amount of spend production had  another key and they simply wanted to  make sure that that key wasn't  distributed widely they didn't want to  keep it inside the source coax and every  developer could access and they didn't  quite know where to put it so the  recommended solution for this and this  is on a public documentation is building  two projects to separate the duties and  actually this is three if you think  about the application that's running as  well so on the left-hand side we have  the secret storage project I'm going to  store them in Google Cloud Storage on  the right hand side we have the cloud  kms project which is the encryption and  decryption and the nice thing here is  that one person or one team can manage  the keys another person in another theme  can manage the encrypted secrets so the  first thing you do is you take your API  key you send it to cloud kms you pick  the particular key in keyring that you  want and it gives you the encrypted blob  to store you then place it in Google  Cloud storage in a bucket as the  encrypted blob when your application  starts up it does two things first it  connects to Google Cloud storage and  reads that blob from the bucket you off  see set up all the permissions and  scopes and roles to be able to pull that  down if any other person had access to  that bucket and logged in and saw it it  would be no use because they couldn't  actually see what's inside that  encrypted blob  so the next step is it goes to cloud KMS  it sends the encrypted blob and it  returns the decrypted version of it and  says here's your API key so what you've  done is you've delinked it you've bought  in separation of the duties someone can  independently rotate the keys and manage  the keys for you  another team commanding encrypted blobs  and secrets then the application can run  fairly stateless Li doesn't need to be  bundled with the secrets every time it  starts from restarts it goes and grabs  that key again and of course this is all  logged so you can start to look for  activity and say well was there any  failed decryption attempts was anyone  pulling down these particular keys from  a different application and a different  source that wasn't then going and  decrypting them you got some pretty good  visibility there so the final solution  I'm going to talk about which is a  really common question is are my  machines up to date and we're talking  about infrastructure here so we're  talking about plain old virtual machines  and the assumption is that they need  updating they need patching and also  they need to conform to your security  standards so there's the baseline that  you define the baseline might be a NIST  or a C is standard and probably your  organization's specific requirements as  well but let's say there's been some  particular vulnerability occur and you  want to update G Lib C or live SSL and  make sure it's maintained so there's two  questions really how do you make sure  people are only using trusted images and  secondly how do you make sure they're  always using the latest version so the  first thing we're going to do is going  to build custom images so part of  compute engine includes an image  registry it's not actually called an  image registry but it is an image  registry inside of compute engine and  with this you can store baked images  typically it's an automated process that  we'll go through and pull down the  patches build them together do your  security configurations and hardening  and publish it could also be a manual  process as well depending on volume and  you can choose whatever flavor you like  particular versions of Linux or Windows  or whatever takes your fancy  and publish those custom images share  them and make them available for other  users and other projects to start with  so in this case a user that has access  to start a virtual machine an instance  pulls the image from the  his registry based on the URL and then  builds it from that version there's a  constraint you can apply in resource  manager that allows you to control which  particular versions and which particular  images can we use and this is important  because by default Google publish a  whole range of images for you we produce  standard Ubuntu standard CentOS standard  Windows Server and that doesn't conform  to your security standards and policies  its passion it's updated and it's fairly  up-to-date with all the vulnerabilities  but it pro doesn't contain your  particular configurations and your  endpoint tools and management tools so  you can apply this constraint and  essentially say none of my users can use  any of the Google provided and libraries  they can use none of the Google product  images is completely denied so what  they're left with is that can only  access the custom versions which you've  published so then what you're saying is  that any developer any person anywhere  in your organization that wants to spin  up an instance has to use an instance  that comes from our custom images which  we've approved and we standardized and  we've pushed out so images go through a  life cycle there's four different states  that they go through by default they're  active you create a new image people can  use it a few days after that you can  deprecated it and you can say that it's  allowed to be used and often people will  support two or three old versions but  they'll get a warning saying that this  is not the latest version and typically  people access images based on the name  or the family they'll say just give me  the latest version of Ubuntu rather than  the particular version that's been  produced but this will tell them  actually the version you just specified  if they've hard-coded in a version  that's not okay obsolete will actually  stop them using it so if they try and  start an instance using that particular  image that's now obsolete it will fail  it won't start up and it will give them  a failure instead of a warning and then  the fourth state is deleted where the  image is flagged as deleted it's not  actually removed from disk you have to  do that separately but it's no longer  visible and available so you can do  automated obsolescence so what this  means is that when you tell image to be  a version of an image to be deprecated  there's two extra flags you can pass  there's an obsolete in and there's a  delete in and this can be a relative  amount of time so in seven days or an  absolute amount of time a particular  date that you give it so as part of your  processes I deal in an automated baking  process you can say right here's the  latest version of an image I've just  published for you and the previous  version deprecated it today so all my  users will get warnings telling them  this is in the latest version and  automatically will then make it obsolete  in seven days time or delete it in 14  days time it's another thing you don't  have to think about it'll just run  through that lifecycle for you and users  should treat it appropriately if people  start complaining that the version of  the image isn't available they need to  change their deployment scripts to use  the image family rather than particular  versions so we talked about three  different things here we talked about  control identity is super important in  any cloud provider particularly google  cloud because all of your access to  resources is focused around your  identity there's much less of a focus on  network controls particularly with more  of the managed services service accounts  are really powerful if you use them  correctly and actually there's lots of  little details around who can access  that service account that's definitely  something I'd recommend you go away and  review probably with full saty  to see who has that control visibility  is out there you can see everything that  happens into our Google cloud but not by  default and you can't wait until there's  an incident to go and find that because  unless the logs are on you can't go  backwards and see it so you need to  review data access logging you need to  review VPC file or logging and if it's  appropriate turn that on today to  prepare you for the future and we talked  a bit about some solutions how do we  combine different DCP services together  to get you to a point that you can run  your applications and services more  securely and get you prepared for a  beyond court world of the future where  all your employees can access remotely  securely and do whatever they need to  thank you very much for attending my  talk I'll be available to take questions  outside  [Applause]  [Music]

